 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "vn-alapin-which-pawn-is-working",
   "line": "c3 c5 e4 Nf6 e5 Nd5 d4 cxd4 Nf3 Nc6 cxd4 g6 Bc4 Nb6 Bb3 d6 Qe2 Bg7 O-O O-O h3 dxe5 dxe5 Qc7 Bf4 Bf5 Nc3 Rfd8 Bxf7+ Kh8 Bb3 Bd3 Qe3 Bxf1 Rxf1 Na5 Be6 Nac4",
   "teaches": "When you are attacking, sort your pawns by what they are doing rather than by whether they can be taken — a pawn that shuts an enemy piece out of the game is part of the attack and must not be traded away, while a pawn on the far side that no piece is using can simply be allowed to fall.",
   "explains": "The queen has to choose a square, and the pawn on b2 is hanging. It can be left: the knight that takes it is out of play, and the attack continues with the knight coming to g5 regardless. What matters far more is the pawn on e5, which is the reason the bishop on g7 has nothing to look at — the queen belongs where it keeps that pawn and the attacking pieces together.",
   "reasons": [
